Pass the `#[link]` libraries and the allocator shim of a Rust link to `cc_common.link`.

Under `experimental_use_cc_common_link`, rustc only emits an object. So the native libraries of the `#[link]`
attributes never reach the linker, and neither does the allocator shim of rustc, which routes `__rust_alloc` to the
`#[global_allocator]` of the crate graph or to the default allocator of std. rustc reads the libraries from the
crate metadata, and it passes both only to the linker that it runs. No other tool can read them.

So let rustc also link each `bin` and `cdylib` with `native_libs_recorder` as `--codegen=linker`. The recorder
writes the libraries from a search path to a response file, and `cc_common.link` takes that file as `@<file>`. It
copies the allocator shim, and on Windows each library that the link takes by path, to a directory that
`cc_common.link` takes with the objects. The objects come before every archive, so the shim defines the allocator
symbols before the link reads the archive of an unused crate with a `#[global_allocator]`. Such a link then needs no
allocator library, and neither does the `CcInfo` of a library, so the link gets no second definition from a
dependency. A `#[global_allocator]` thus needs no `experimental_use_global_allocator`.

The `experimental_cc_common_link_native_libs_recorder` setting names the recorder, and `//rust/private/cc:empty`
turns the feature off. The toolchain reads it, so that a library sees it too. The recorder would otherwise depend
on itself, so it is built without the recorder and without `cc_common.link`: rustc links it and its tools itself.

A `link.exe` link takes some libraries by path: an import library that rustc writes for a `raw-dylib` import in a
temporary directory, or one that a build script finds, such as `windows.0.52.0.lib`. The recorder writes a copy of
the first object as the link output, so rustc can strip it after the link. On Apple, rustc also runs `dsymutil` on
a packed link output, so the action sets `split-debuginfo=unpacked` there. That does not change the object.
